Yes—in the broad historical sense, the Japanese military’s “comfort women” system involved forced mobilization and sexual coercion. The term “comfort women” is itself a euphemism for women and girls, mostly from Korea, China, Southeast Asia, and other occupied areas, who were used sexually by Japanese soldiers before and during World War II.

The important nuance is this: **not every woman was literally rounded up at gunpoint by Japanese soldiers**, and recruitment varied by time and place. But mainstream historical scholarship concludes that coercion was pervasive and that the Japanese military was directly or indirectly involved in establishing, regulating, and maintaining the “comfort station” system. Women were obtained through:

- deception and false promises of work;
- abduction and trafficking by civilian brokers operating with military permission or supervision;
- debt bondage and sale by families under extreme poverty;
- outright force and intimidation;
- confinement and inability to leave once in the stations.

So if “forcibly mobilized” means **“many women were moved into sexual servitude against their will, under conditions of coercion and military control,” the answer is yes.** The Japanese government’s 1993 Kono Statement acknowledged military involvement and that women were recruited “against their will” in many cases through coaxing, coercion, and other means. Some conservative Japanese accounts dispute the extent of direct military force, but the mainstream historical consensus is that the system was coercive and amounted to sexual slavery.

Yes—many were, though the historical record is more varied than a single phrase can capture.

The Japanese military’s “comfort women” system was a state/military-organized and military-supervised system of sexual exploitation. Women and girls from Korea, China, Taiwan, the Philippines, Indonesia, and other areas—as well as Japan itself—were recruited or transferred into military brothels. Recruitment was carried out through a mixture of:

- outright abduction or rounding up by soldiers or local authorities in some cases;
- deception by recruiters promising factory or nursing work;
- debt bondage and trafficking by private brokers operating with military permission;
- coercion under colonial occupation and wartime conditions;
- and, in some cases, apparent voluntary recruitment—though even “voluntary” cases often involved severe economic coercion or misleading offers.

So the accurate statement is not that every single woman was literally seized by Japanese troops, but that coercion and force were widespread and central to the system, and the Japanese military was deeply involved in establishing, contracting, transporting, policing, and using the brothels. The 1993 Japanese government “Kono Statement” acknowledged military involvement and coercion in recruitment. Historians broadly agree that many women were forcibly mobilized or trafficked; the exact number remains disputed, and denialist claims that the system was entirely voluntary are not supported by the evidence.
